<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ptolemy Keraunos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 319–<a href="/years/279-bce">279 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Ptolemy Ceraunus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ptolemy Keraunos was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> who was passed over for the Egyptian succession. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> at Corupedium in 281 BCE, Ptolemy Keraunos assassinated him. The murder prevented Seleucus from uniting Alexander's former territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ptolemy-keraunos.jpg" alt="Ptolemy Keraunos" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stater_of_Ptolemy_Keraunos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ptolemy was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> and his first wife Eurydice, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a>, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and was probably born in 319 BCE. His epithet Keraunos is Greek for thunderbolt and referred to an impatient, impetuous and destructive character. Between 317 and 314 BCE his father married Berenice, one of Eurydice's ladies-in-waiting, and had further children, among them the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a>. At first Ptolemy was heir presumptive, but as Berenice's son grew older a power struggle developed between the half-brothers, and Ptolemy left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around 287 BCE. Ptolemy II was made co-regent on 28 March 284 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ptolemy went to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. The court was divided: Lysimachus was married to Ptolemy II's full sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsinoe-ii">Arsinoe II</a>, while his heir Agathocles was married to Ptolemy's full sister Lysandra. The sisters were already in conflict over the succession. Lysimachus chose to back Ptolemy II. When Agathocles was executed in 282 BCE, the historian Memnon names Ptolemy as the murderer, but all other sources place him on Agathocles' side and have him accompany Lysandra to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>. Seleucus invaded early in 281 BCE and Lysimachus was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-corupedium">Battle of Corupedium</a>.</p><p>Ptolemy came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus</a>' control and joined his inner circle. In September 281 BCE, as Seleucus sacrificed at a place called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> before crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, Ptolemy murdered him, intending to take the territories of his protector. He was thus responsible for the death of the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diadochi">Diadochi</a>, Alexander's successors. He was acclaimed king by part of Seleucus' army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a> and formally gave up his claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As king of Macedon Ptolemy defeated Antigonos Gonatas in a naval battle and confined him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a>, which freed Pyrrhus to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Justin reports that he gave Pyrrhus troops and elephants and a daughter in marriage, though some scholars doubt this. He proposed marriage to his half-sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsinoe-ii">Arsinoe II</a>, widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, who agreed on condition that her young sons be kept safe. On the wedding day he murdered the two younger sons, and she fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Most of 280 BCE was taken up by a war with Arsinoe's eldest son, probably Ptolemy Epigonos, and an Illyrian king, Monunius.</p><p>In January or February 279 BCE Gauls led by Bolgius invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Ptolemy refused to wait for his full force and rejected help from 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardanians</a>. He was wounded and captured and the Gauls killed him. After his death anarchy followed until Antigonos Gonatas defeated the Gauls near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a> in 277 BCE and was recognised as king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ptolemy Ceraunus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within months, before he could consolidate this position, he was assassinated by <b>Ptolemy Ceraunus</b>, an early instance of the dynastic instability that marked the empire throughout its history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
